SEC10 = [
 (None, [("Table 12 condenses our results into seven guidelines for authors of appearance-based gaze datasets. Each guideline targets one way in which a release can leak identity or overstate its usefulness. The full checklist and the audit tool are provided as supplementary material ", False), ("[18]", True), (".", False)]),
 ("Measuring privacy (G1, G2).", [(" A single privacy figure hides which question it answers. Matching against photographs (A1) and grouping within the release (A2) disagree by up to 60 points on the same crops, so both have to be reported, and the face crop has to be tested as well as the eye crops (G1). Neither number can be interpreted without a floor. On single-session recordings a face-free patch already reaches a Rank-1 of 50.5%, so a floor measured on the same frames is the only reference against which leakage can be read (G2).", False)]),
 ("What the release contains (G3 to G5).", [(" Genuine eye crops restore accuracy because they are the participant's own biometric, so a protocol that keeps them buys utility with identity (G3). Crop size and photometry link participants without any face model, and both can be closed at almost no cost with fixed crop sizes and per-crop normalisation (G4). For releases that remove participant identifiers, the two-part condition states what a generator has to achieve. Identity must not be held fixed per participant, and every released pixel must lie inside the generator's edit region (G5).", False)]),
 ("Measuring utility and deciding on release (G6, G7).", [(" Utility should be reported for models trained on the release and tested on real faces, beside the no-information reference. The pupil should also be checked directly, because a release can transfer well while carrying less gaze information than another (G6). When any check fails, the release should go to controlled access together with a statement of which channels leak (G7).", False)]),
 ("The privacy–utility plane.", [(" Figure 7 is the instrument behind these guidelines, and it differs from earlier evaluations in two ways. First, both axes are measured on the release and under the conditions in which it would be used. The horizontal axis is the D→R penalty, the cost to a third party who trains on the release and deploys on real users, read against the initialisation noise floor and the no-information reference. The vertical axis is the linkage of the most linkable released crop, read against a floor measured on the same recordings. Earlier evaluations usually report utility under R→D and privacy as matching against the real face, and both choices can make a release look better than it is. Second, every method, release form and strength setting sits in the same frame. The plane therefore shows directly whether any protocol reaches the private-and-useful corner, and how protocols move when they are changed. Restoring the original eyes returns a point to full linkage, and raising the FAMS strength trades pupil signal for identity protection without lowering linkage. Other dataset authors can place their own releases on the same plane with privaudit and the retraining protocol.", False)]),
]
